1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,960
Kevlar is poly-para-phenylene
terephthalamide.

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:07,916
That name describes the structure:
repeating units of benzene rings linked by

3
00:00:07,916 --> 00:00:08,688
amide bonds.

4
00:00:08,688 --> 00:00:13,698
DuPont chemist Stephanie Kwolek
synthesized it in 1965 while searching for

5
00:00:13,698 --> 00:00:17,760
a lightweight strong fiber to replace
steel in radial tires.

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:23,077
The strength comes not from exotic atoms
but from how the polymer chains align.

7
00:00:23,077 --> 00:00:28,418
What makes Kevlar different from nylon,
which shares the amide linkage, is the

8
00:00:28,418 --> 00:00:33,280
rigid aromatic backbone that forces the
molecules into parallel arrays.

9
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:37,664
The question is not what elements it
contains but how those elements are

10
00:00:37,664 --> 00:00:42,779
arranged and why that arrangement produces
properties steel cannot match at the same

11
00:00:42,779 --> 00:00:43,205
weight.

12
00:00:43,205 --> 00:00:45,067
What is Kevlar made of?

13
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:48,382
The synthesis starts with two small
molecules.

14
00:00:48,382 --> 00:00:53,787
Para-phenylenediamine is a benzene ring
with amine groups at opposite ends.

15
00:00:53,787 --> 00:00:58,906
Terephthaloyl chloride is another benzene
ring with acid chloride groups at opposite

16
00:00:58,906 --> 00:00:59,211
ends.

17
00:00:59,211 --> 00:01:03,639
When mixed in a solvent like
N-methyl-pyrrolidone, the amine groups

18
00:01:03,639 --> 00:01:08,266
react with the acid chloride groups,
forming amide bonds and releasing

19
00:01:08,266 --> 00:01:09,456
hydrochloric acid.

20
00:01:09,456 --> 00:01:14,059
The reaction proceeds at room temperature,
which is unusual for polymer synthesis.

21
00:01:14,059 --> 00:01:16,854
Most high-performance polymers require
heat.

22
00:01:16,854 --> 00:01:21,874
The chains grow rapidly, and because the
benzene rings are locked in a straight

23
00:01:21,874 --> 00:01:25,115
line by the amide bonds, the polymer does
not coil.

24
00:01:25,115 --> 00:01:29,728
Instead, the stiff chains align into a
liquid crystalline phase while still

25
00:01:29,728 --> 00:01:33,419
dissolved. This phase is the key to
everything that follows.

26
00:01:33,419 --> 00:01:36,960
A liquid crystal is a state between liquid
and solid.

27
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:42,372
The polymer chains move past each other
like a liquid, but they maintain parallel

28
00:01:42,372 --> 00:01:44,176
orientation like a crystal.

29
00:01:44,176 --> 00:01:46,690
This alignment in solution is critical.

30
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:51,008
It means the chains are already organized
before the fiber is spun.

31
00:01:51,008 --> 00:01:55,335
Nylon chains, by contrast, are flexible
and coil randomly in solution.

32
00:01:55,335 --> 00:01:58,981
Kevlar's rigidity comes from the
para-linked benzene rings.

33
00:01:58,981 --> 00:02:03,266
If the rings were meta-linked, with bonds
at adjacent positions instead of opposite

34
00:02:03,266 --> 00:02:07,189
positions, the chain would kink and the
liquid crystal phase would not form.

35
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:10,046
That variant exists and is called Nomex.

36
00:02:10,046 --> 00:02:14,117
It has heat resistance but not Kevlar's
tensile strength.

37
00:02:14,117 --> 00:02:18,564
The difference between para and meta
linkage is a single bond angle, but it

38
00:02:18,564 --> 00:02:21,707
determines whether the material can bear
load or not.

39
00:02:21,707 --> 00:02:25,994
The liquid crystalline solution is
extruded through a spinneret into a

40
00:02:25,994 --> 00:02:27,035
coagulation bath.

41
00:02:27,035 --> 00:02:31,739
The solvent diffuses out and the polymer
solidifies into a fiber.

42
00:02:31,739 --> 00:02:37,601
During extrusion, shear forces align the
chains even more tightly along the fiber

43
00:02:37,601 --> 00:02:37,963
axis.

44
00:02:37,963 --> 00:02:42,104
The resulting fiber has a high degree of
molecular orientation.

45
00:02:42,104 --> 00:02:47,429
X-ray diffraction studies show that the
chains pack into crystalline regions with

46
00:02:47,429 --> 00:02:50,651
the benzene rings stacked parallel to each
other.

47
00:02:50,651 --> 00:02:55,163
Hydrogen bonds form between the amide
groups on adjacent chains, locking them

48
00:02:55,163 --> 00:02:55,691
together.

49
00:02:55,691 --> 00:02:59,669
This cross-linking through hydrogen bonds
is why Kevlar does not melt.

50
00:02:59,669 --> 00:03:04,101
Heating breaks the hydrogen bonds and
degrades the polymer before it can flow.

51
00:03:04,101 --> 00:03:06,435
The crystalline regions are not perfect.

52
00:03:06,435 --> 00:03:11,393
There are amorphous zones where chains are
less ordered, but the overall alignment is

53
00:03:11,393 --> 00:03:15,826
high enough that most of the load is
carried by the covalent backbone rather

54
00:03:15,826 --> 00:03:17,984
than by weaker intermolecular forces.

55
00:03:17,984 --> 00:03:24,485
Kevlar 49, the high-modulus variant, has a
tensile strength around 3.6 gigapascals.

56
00:03:24,485 --> 00:03:30,389
Steel wire of comparable weight has a
tensile strength near 1.5 gigapascals.

57
00:03:30,389 --> 00:03:33,051
Nylon 6 reaches about 0.08 gigapascals.

58
00:03:33,051 --> 00:03:38,032
The difference is not the strength of
individual bonds but the alignment.

59
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:43,412
In Kevlar, nearly all the chains run along
the fiber axis, so a tensile load pulls

60
00:03:43,412 --> 00:03:45,643
directly on the covalent backbone.

61
00:03:45,643 --> 00:03:50,673
In nylon, the chains are less aligned and
the load is distributed across weaker van

62
00:03:50,673 --> 00:03:53,157
der Waals forces between coiled segments.

63
00:03:53,157 --> 00:03:57,921
The strength-to-weight advantage over
steel is what made Kevlar viable for body

64
00:03:57,921 --> 00:04:01,056
armor and aerospace applications where
mass matters.

65
00:04:01,056 --> 00:04:05,199
Aromatic means the polymer backbone
contains benzene rings.

66
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:10,747
Aliphatic polymers like polyethylene have
chains of carbon atoms without rings.

67
00:04:10,747 --> 00:04:15,738
Aromatic rings are planar and rigid, and
the electrons are delocalized across the

68
00:04:15,738 --> 00:04:18,019
ring, which stabilizes the structure.

69
00:04:18,019 --> 00:04:23,634
Kevlar resists most acids, bases, and
organic solvents at room temperature.

70
00:04:23,634 --> 00:04:29,325
Concentrated sulfuric acid will dissolve
it, which is actually how Kevlar is

71
00:04:29,325 --> 00:04:31,347
recycled in some processes.

72
00:04:31,347 --> 00:04:36,416
The aramid structure is stable up to about
500 degrees Celsius in inert atmosphere,

73
00:04:36,416 --> 00:04:40,387
though it degrades in air at lower
temperatures due to oxidation.

74
00:04:40,387 --> 00:04:44,562
The chemical resistance comes from the
stability of the amide bond and the

75
00:04:44,562 --> 00:04:48,285
aromatic rings, both of which require
significant energy to break.

76
00:04:48,285 --> 00:04:54,001
Published degradation temperatures vary
depending on atmosphere and heating rate.

77
00:04:54,001 --> 00:04:59,928
In nitrogen, onset of decomposition is
reported between 450 and 550 degrees

78
00:04:59,928 --> 00:05:06,173
In air, oxidation begins around 300
degrees Celsius. The fiber does not melt.

79
00:05:06,173 --> 00:05:10,793
Thermogravimetric analysis shows mass loss
starting at these temperatures,

80
00:05:10,793 --> 00:05:12,728
corresponding to bond cleavage.

81
00:05:12,728 --> 00:05:17,492
This thermal stability is why aramid
fibers are used in firefighter gear and

82
00:05:17,492 --> 00:05:18,933
aerospace applications.

83
00:05:18,933 --> 00:05:23,408
The lack of a melting point is both an
advantage and a limitation.

84
00:05:23,408 --> 00:05:28,806
It means the fiber retains its structure
at high temperatures, but it also means

85
00:05:28,806 --> 00:05:32,314
Kevlar cannot be melt-processed like
thermoplastics.

86
00:05:32,314 --> 00:05:36,160
Once the fiber is made, it cannot be
reshaped by heating.

87
00:05:36,160 --> 00:05:39,680
Kevlar degrades under prolonged
ultraviolet exposure.

88
00:05:39,680 --> 00:05:43,333
UV photons have enough energy to break the
amide bonds.

89
00:05:43,333 --> 00:05:47,433
Chain scission reduces molecular weight
and disrupts the alignment.

90
00:05:47,433 --> 00:05:50,187
The fiber yellows and loses tensile
strength.

91
00:05:50,187 --> 00:05:52,045
This is a known limitation.

92
00:05:52,045 --> 00:05:57,552
Kevlar used outdoors is typically coated
or embedded in a matrix that blocks UV.

93
00:05:57,552 --> 00:06:02,874
The degradation is surface-limited; the
core of a thick yarn remains intact longer

94
00:06:02,874 --> 00:06:04,301
than the outer layers.

95
00:06:04,301 --> 00:06:09,262
Accelerated weathering tests show
measurable strength loss after hundreds of

96
00:06:09,262 --> 00:06:12,787
hours of UV exposure equivalent to months
of sunlight.

97
00:06:12,787 --> 00:06:15,648
The rate depends on wavelength and
intensity.

98
00:06:15,648 --> 00:06:20,863
Near-UV around 300 to 400 nanometers is
the most damaging range because it matches

99
00:06:20,863 --> 00:06:23,533
the energy needed to break the amide bond.

100
00:06:23,533 --> 00:06:29,645
Coatings that absorb or reflect UV extend
the service life, but they add weight and

101
00:06:29,645 --> 00:06:30,013
cost.

102
00:06:30,013 --> 00:06:34,147
Every strength in this fiber comes with
its exact opposite built in.

103
00:06:34,147 --> 00:06:38,798
The same rigid, aligned chains that make
Kevlar strong under tension are what make

104
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:40,272
it weak under compression.

105
00:06:40,272 --> 00:06:44,243
I couldn't state the strength without
stating the trade-off beside it.

106
00:06:44,243 --> 00:06:46,274
DuPont produces several grades.

107
00:06:46,274 --> 00:06:51,452
Kevlar 29 has lower modulus and higher
elongation, used in ropes and body armor

108
00:06:51,452 --> 00:06:53,549
where energy absorption matters.

109
00:06:53,549 --> 00:06:58,715
Kevlar 49 has higher modulus and lower
elongation, used in composites where

110
00:06:58,715 --> 00:06:59,955
stiffness matters.

111
00:06:59,955 --> 00:07:03,451
Kevlar 129 has improved toughness through
modified processing.

112
00:07:03,451 --> 00:07:07,680
The base polymer is the same; the
differences come from spinning conditions

113
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:12,248
and heat treatment that adjust the degree
of crystallinity and chain orientation.

114
00:07:12,248 --> 00:07:17,208
Higher orientation increases modulus but
reduces elongation to break.

115
00:07:17,208 --> 00:07:19,293
The trade-off is fundamental.

116
00:07:19,293 --> 00:07:23,615
You can optimize for stiffness or for
energy absorption, but not both

117
00:07:23,615 --> 00:07:26,371
simultaneously with the same
microstructure.

118
00:07:26,371 --> 00:07:30,750
Kevlar 49 elongates about 2.5 percent
before breaking.

119
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:33,832
Steel wire elongates around 1 percent.

120
00:07:33,832 --> 00:07:35,945
Nylon elongates 15 to 30 percent.

121
00:07:35,945 --> 00:07:40,427
Low elongation means Kevlar does not
stretch much under load, which is

122
00:07:40,427 --> 00:07:44,205
desirable in applications like cables and
pressure vessels.

123
00:07:44,205 --> 00:07:49,087
High elongation, as in nylon, allows
energy absorption through deformation,

124
00:07:49,087 --> 00:07:51,496
which is desirable in climbing ropes.

125
00:07:51,496 --> 00:07:57,882
Body armor uses Kevlar 29 because the
slightly higher elongation helps dissipate

126
00:07:57,882 --> 00:07:59,000
impact energy.

127
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:03,779
The fabric deforms enough to spread the
load over a larger area, reducing the peak

128
00:08:03,779 --> 00:08:05,527
stress at the point of impact.

129
00:08:05,527 --> 00:08:09,373
A stiffer fiber would concentrate the load
and fail more abruptly.

130
00:08:09,373 --> 00:08:11,880
Kevlar is rarely used as a single fiber.

131
00:08:11,880 --> 00:08:16,643
It is woven into fabric or combined with a
resin matrix to form a composite.

132
00:08:16,643 --> 00:08:22,183
In a composite, the Kevlar carries tensile
loads and the resin transfers load between

133
00:08:22,183 --> 00:08:24,595
fibers and protects them from damage.

134
00:08:24,595 --> 00:08:26,220
Epoxy is a common matrix.

135
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:31,484
The fabric is laid into a mold, resin is
applied, and the assembly is cured under

136
00:08:31,484 --> 00:08:32,589
heat or pressure.

137
00:08:32,589 --> 00:08:34,428
Vacuum bagging removes trapped air.

138
00:08:34,428 --> 00:08:38,315
The resulting laminate is stiff in the
plane of the fabric but weak in the

139
00:08:38,315 --> 00:08:42,675
through-thickness direction because there
are fewer fibers crossing between layers.

140
00:08:42,675 --> 00:08:47,715
Delamination, where layers separate, is a
common failure mode in Kevlar composites.

141
00:08:47,715 --> 00:08:52,467
Stitching or z-pinning can improve
through-thickness strength by mechanically

142
00:08:52,467 --> 00:08:53,640
linking the layers.

143
00:08:53,640 --> 00:08:56,995
Kevlar is strong in tension but weak in
compression.

144
00:08:56,995 --> 00:08:59,704
Under compressive load, the fibers buckle.

145
00:08:59,704 --> 00:09:04,262
This is a consequence of the structure:
the chains are held together by hydrogen

146
00:09:04,262 --> 00:09:08,763
bonds perpendicular to the fiber axis, but
those bonds are much weaker than the

147
00:09:08,763 --> 00:09:10,643
covalent backbone along the axis.

148
00:09:10,643 --> 00:09:13,590
In compression, the chains can slide past
each other.

149
00:09:13,590 --> 00:09:18,039
Composites that need compressive strength
often hybrid Kevlar with carbon fiber,

150
00:09:18,039 --> 00:09:19,875
which resists compression better.

151
00:09:19,875 --> 00:09:22,097
Carbon fiber has a different structure.

152
00:09:22,097 --> 00:09:26,884
The graphite planes are stacked and bonded
by van der Waals forces, but the in-plane

153
00:09:26,884 --> 00:09:29,619
covalent bonds give it high compressive
modulus.

154
00:09:29,619 --> 00:09:34,182
Combining the two fibers in a single
laminate allows the designer to tailor

155
00:09:34,182 --> 00:09:36,312
properties in different directions.

156
00:09:36,312 --> 00:09:39,482
The strength is not in the atoms but in
the alignment.

157
00:09:39,482 --> 00:09:44,179
Kevlar and nylon have similar bond types,
but Kevlar's rigid backbone forces the

158
00:09:44,179 --> 00:09:47,467
chains into parallel arrays that bear load
collectively.

159
00:09:47,467 --> 00:09:51,753
The liquid crystalline phase during
synthesis is the enabling step.

160
00:09:51,753 --> 00:09:57,062
Without it, the chains would solidify in
random orientations and the fiber would be

161
00:09:57,062 --> 00:09:58,533
no stronger than nylon.

162
00:09:58,533 --> 00:10:03,602
The hydrogen bonds between chains prevent
melting and add lateral cohesion, but the

163
00:10:03,602 --> 00:10:06,656
tensile strength comes from the covalent
backbone.

164
00:10:06,656 --> 00:10:11,107
UV degradation and compressive weakness
are both consequences of the same

165
00:10:11,107 --> 00:10:13,424
structure that gives tensile strength.

166
00:10:13,424 --> 00:10:18,312
The rigidity that aligns the chains also
makes them vulnerable to bond cleavage

167
00:10:18,312 --> 00:10:18,869
under UV.

168
00:10:18,869 --> 00:10:23,186
The parallel packing that maximizes
tensile strength also allows buckling

169
00:10:23,186 --> 00:10:24,251
under compression.

170
00:10:24,251 --> 00:10:29,094
There is no version of this material that
eliminates all limitations.

171
00:10:29,094 --> 00:10:31,200
Every optimization is a trade.

172
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:36,987
Tensile strength values vary with test
method, fiber batch, and conditioning.

173
00:10:36,987 --> 00:10:41,872
Published values for Kevlar 49 range from
3.4 to 3.8 gigapascals.

174
00:10:41,872 --> 00:10:45,735
The degradation temperature depends on
atmosphere and heating rate.

175
00:10:45,735 --> 00:10:50,635
The synthesis details are proprietary; the
open literature describes the reaction but

176
00:10:50,635 --> 00:10:54,325
not the exact solvent blends or spinning
parameters DuPont uses.

177
00:10:54,325 --> 00:10:59,381
What is certain is the polymer identity,
the liquid crystalline processing, and the

178
00:10:59,381 --> 00:11:00,965
alignment-driven strength.

179
00:11:00,965 --> 00:11:05,793
The rest is optimization that varies by
manufacturer and application.

180
00:11:05,793 --> 00:11:11,040
The fundamental chemistry is known. The
engineering is still being refined.

181
00:11:11,040 --> 00:11:16,299
Kevlar is a demonstration that material
properties emerge from structure as much

182
00:11:16,299 --> 00:11:17,285
as composition.

183
00:11:17,285 --> 00:11:20,382
The same elements arranged differently
give nylon.

184
00:11:20,382 --> 00:11:25,397
The same polymer processed differently
gives Nomex. The strength is in the order.
